WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.585 --> 00:00:09.091
The reign of King Henry VIII of England was plagued by controversy.

2
00:00:09.752 --> 00:00:17.318
Catherine of Aragon, Henry's wife, gave birth to six children, but only Mary survived through infancy, leaving Henry

3
00:00:17.358 --> 00:00:18.319
with no male heir.

4
00:00:19.139 --> 00:00:24.944
He blamed Catherine and sought to dissolve their union in order to marry another woman—one who could bear

5
00:00:24.984 --> 00:00:25.484
him a son.

6
00:00:26.525 --> 00:00:32.829
The Roman Catholic pope, however, refused to annul his marriage, so Henry broke the English Church away from

7
00:00:32.869 --> 00:00:35.530
the Catholic Church, led by the pope in Rome.

8
00:00:36.891 --> 00:00:44.714
In many nations, new Protestant churches were being established as the Reformation—a religious and political movement defying Rome—swept

9
00:00:44.794 --> 00:00:45.494
across Europe.

10
00:00:46.235 --> 00:00:51.717
In England, the popes' claim to jurisdiction over the country had been a long-running source of dispute.

11
00:00:52.197 --> 00:00:57.559
In a pivotal moment of the English Reformation, Henry's break with Rome resulted in the formation of a

12
00:00:57.599 --> 00:00:59.600
new church, the Church of England.

13
00:01:00.628 --> 00:01:05.910
Henry declared his marriage to Catherine invalid, and England's new church recognized the divorce.

14
00:01:07.611 --> 00:01:13.254
When Henry remarried, it was to Anne Boleyn, a well-known and influential woman in his court.

15
00:01:14.594 --> 00:01:20.337
As queen, she stripped Mary of her title and ordered her to serve Elizabeth, her own newborn daughter

16
00:01:20.377 --> 00:01:20.937
by Henry.

17
00:01:21.858 --> 00:01:26.800
But when Anne also failed to provide a male heir, Henry had her beheaded for adultery.

18
00:01:27.648 --> 00:01:33.934
Henry married again; this time, his wife, Jane Seymour, died soon after giving birth to a boy, Edward.

19
00:01:34.874 --> 00:01:40.339
But Edward seemed frail, and Henry remarried three more times in the hopes of fathering another son.

20
00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.942
None of these marriages, however, produced a male child.

21
00:01:44.723 --> 00:01:48.386
When Henry died in 1547, Edward became king.

22
00:01:49.307 --> 00:01:53.811
He had been raised Protestant, and he continued his father's break with Catholicism.

23
00:01:54.611 --> 00:02:01.638
However, Edward ruled for only six years before he died of tuberculosis; Mary, who had remained a devout

24
00:02:01.698 --> 00:02:03.258
Catholic, then took the throne.

25
00:02:04.359 --> 00:02:10.620
Mary was determined to repair relations with the pope and restore Catholicism as the official religion of England.

26
00:02:11.741 --> 00:02:17.362
To this end, she wed King Philip II of Spain, a champion of Catholicism in Europe.

27
00:02:18.203 --> 00:02:22.664
Many of her subjects, particularly the English Protestants, were unhappy with the marriage.

28
00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.864
Sir Thomas Wyatt the Younger, a soldier, organized a rebellion in the predominantly Protestant north.

29
00:02:29.785 --> 00:02:34.928
The rebellion moved into London and grew to more than three thousand men before it was stopped.

30
00:02:35.809 --> 00:02:40.172
After this rebellion, Mary began to fear that everyone was plotting against her.

31
00:02:41.093 --> 00:02:46.977
She even had her half sister, Elizabeth, imprisoned in the Tower of London while her ministers looked for

32
00:02:47.037 --> 00:02:48.999
a connection between Elizabeth and Wyatt.

33
00:02:49.839 --> 00:02:54.317
After two months, Mary released Elizabeth but kept her under house arrest.

34
00:02:55.418 --> 00:03:02.181
Mary's attempts to restore Catholicism in England led to the persecution of Protestants and other opponents of Catholicism.

35
00:03:03.081 --> 00:03:08.604
Thousands were burned at the stake for heresy, which earned the queen the unwelcome nickname "Bloody Mary."

36
00:03:09.384 --> 00:03:15.527
Her husband had returned to Spain soon after their marriage, and at his request, she supplied English soldiers

37
00:03:15.587 --> 00:03:17.408
to support Spain's war with France.

38
00:03:18.416 --> 00:03:24.260
England's involvement in this war led to the loss of the last English territory on the European continent,

39
00:03:24.961 --> 00:03:26.261
the port city of Calais.

40
00:03:27.242 --> 00:03:29.784
This loss did not endear Mary to her subjects.

41
00:03:30.544 --> 00:03:33.586
She became depressed and her health deteriorated.

42
00:03:34.407 --> 00:03:38.530
After only five years of rule, Mary died without leaving behind an heir.

43
00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:42.713
The next person in the line of succession was her half sister, Elizabeth.

44
00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:47.536
When Elizabeth assumed the throne, no one knew where her allegiances lay.

45
00:03:48.188 --> 00:03:52.733
Elizabeth had grown up Protestant but had attended Catholic services during Mary's reign.

46
00:03:53.654 --> 00:03:56.577
Elizabeth's competency to rule also came into question.

47
00:03:57.457 --> 00:04:02.723
It was a common belief at the time that women based decisions solely on their emotions, not reason

48
00:04:02.803 --> 00:04:06.026
or logic, and thus were not fit to command countries.

49
00:04:07.087 --> 00:04:11.932
Elizabeth, however, had been educated in classical languages, rhetoric, history, and philosophy.

50
00:04:12.692 --> 00:04:18.797
She responded to these objections by stating that, although she was female, she acquired a new, genderless body

51
00:04:18.857 --> 00:04:19.938
when she became queen.

52
00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.862
Elizabeth's rule over England was long, dramatic, and controversial.

53
00:04:25.623 --> 00:04:30.927
One of her first acts as queen was to reinstate the Church of England as the official religion

54
00:04:30.967 --> 00:04:31.648
of her kingdom.

55
00:04:32.309 --> 00:04:38.914
She passed the Act of Uniformity in 1559, which commanded that church services use the Protestant Book of

56
00:04:38.974 --> 00:04:43.722
Common Prayer, which was in English, whereas the Catholic Church still used Latin.

57
00:04:44.742 --> 00:04:49.426
Some Protestants were pleased with these developments, but others thought they did not go far enough.

58
00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:54.529
During her reign, Elizabeth dealt with rebellions led by Catholics.

59
00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:59.332
Several plots to assassinate her were discovered before they could be put into action.

60
00:05:00.313 --> 00:05:05.016
The Ridolfi Plot involved her cousin Mary Stuart—better known as Mary, Queen of Scots.

61
00:05:05.897 --> 00:05:10.120
Mary was a Catholic and therefore supported by the pope in her claim to the English crown.

62
00:05:11.028 --> 00:05:17.211
Roberto Ridolfi, an Italian banker living in London and a devout Catholic, believed that the only way to

63
00:05:17.251 --> 00:05:22.534
return Catholicism to England was to assassinate Elizabeth and put Mary Stuart on the throne.

64
00:05:23.575 --> 00:05:30.719
But Elizabeth's intelligence service uncovered the plot in 1571, and most of the guilty were put to death—although

65
00:05:30.799 --> 00:05:32.840
Elizabeth refused to execute Mary.

66
00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:40.004
Only after Mary Stuart was implicated in another assassination plot did Elizabeth agree to put her cousin to

67
00:05:40.044 --> 00:05:46.896
death. Despite the controversies of Elizabeth's reign, her subjects had many reasons to celebrate.

68
00:05:47.457 --> 00:05:53.222
The Elizabethan period later came to be known as the English Renaissance because the arts flourished during this

69
00:05:53.262 --> 00:05:58.426
time. William Shakespeare wrote most of his plays while Elizabeth was on the throne.

70
00:05:59.327 --> 00:06:04.431
She hired dozens of musicians and composers to create music for the court and the English people.

71
00:06:05.431 --> 00:06:07.153
There were military victories as well.

72
00:06:07.773 --> 00:06:13.401
Sir Francis Drake, a privateer supported by Elizabeth, led raids on Spanish ports and trading ships.

73
00:06:14.441 --> 00:06:19.484
And when Philip II of Spain, her former brother-in-law, attacked England in order to add it to the

74
00:06:19.524 --> 00:06:26.987
Spanish Empire and return it to Catholicism, his supposedly invincible navy, known as "the Armada," proved no match

75
00:06:27.067 --> 00:06:27.947
for the English fleet.

76
00:06:29.128 --> 00:06:33.009
Like her half sister, Mary, Elizabeth remained childless throughout her reign.

77
00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:38.511
Many of her advisors tried to convince her to take a husband and produce an heir to resolve

78
00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:39.572
the issue of succession.

79
00:06:40.476 --> 00:06:46.179
But Elizabeth refused to marry; she proclaimed that she was married to her kingdom and responsible for its

80
00:06:46.219 --> 00:06:48.761
people. These were not empty words.

81
00:06:49.541 --> 00:06:55.444
Before the English victory over the Spanish Armada, Elizabeth had personally reviewed her troops in spite of the

82
00:06:55.484 --> 00:07:02.308
danger. When her advisors protested, Elizabeth responded by saying, "I know I have the body of a weak

83
00:07:02.328 --> 00:07:06.611
and feeble woman, but I have the heart and stomach of a king, and of a king of

84
00:07:06.691 --> 00:07:07.271
England too."

85
00:07:07.951 --> 00:07:13.848
Beloved by her people, Good Queen Bess, as she became known, ruled for over 44 years until her

86
00:07:13.888 --> 00:07:15.169
death in 1603.

87
00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:21.614
One might expect that their common upbringing would lead Bloody Mary and Good Queen Bess to hold similar

88
00:07:21.654 --> 00:07:24.737
values and adopt similar ways of ruling the country.

89
00:07:25.417 --> 00:07:29.340
The reigns of these two queens, however, could not have been more different.

90
00:07:30.001 --> 00:07:34.264
Their responses to events during their reigns had contrasting effects on the country.

91
00:07:34.965 --> 00:07:40.150
But even though they differed hugely in both personalities and beliefs, they shared a love of England.

92
00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:47.277
Perhaps this is why they are interred together in Westminster Abbey under this inscription: "Partners both in throne

93
00:07:47.297 --> 00:07:52.762
and grave, here rest we two sisters, Elizabeth and Mary, in the hope of one resurrection."
